The dependency options reach the -E step that reads the interface, and
the rule names the header as its target and the interface and each file
that it includes as its prerequisites. Under -MD and -MMD it goes beside
the header with the suffix .d after the header's own, unless -MF names
another file; under -M and -MM it goes to stdout, or to the file that
-MF names, and no header is written. -MT and -MQ name another target,
and "-MF -" is stdout. The interface includes no system header, and
-nostdinc keeps the backend from adding one of its own, as GCC adds
stdc-predef.h on glibc, so the rules of -MD and -M name the files that
those of -MMD and -MM do. own_rules leaves out the files of the host
that a backend names all the same (dependency_rules.sh).

  $ . ./dependency_rules.sh
  $ header() {
  >   typec --cc="$cc" -nostdinc -Wno-trigraphs --emit-c-header "$@"
  > }
  $ for cc in "$clang" "$gcc"; do
  >   header -MD room.tci && head -1 room.h && own_rules < room.h.d
  >   header -MMD -MP room.tci -o elsewhere.h && own_rules < elsewhere.h.d
  >   header -MD -MF rule.d -MT 'room $(h)' room.tci && own_rules < rule.d
  >   mv room.h kept.h
  >   header -MM -MQ 'room $(h)' room.tci | own_rules
  >   header -M -MF rule.d room.tci && own_rules < rule.d
  >   test -e room.h || echo no header
  >   header -MMD -MQ room.h -MF - room.tci -o - |
  >     sed -n '/^room\.h:/,$p' | own_rules
  > done
  /* Generated by typec --emit-c-header. Do not edit. */
  room.h: room.tci cap.h base.tci
  elsewhere.h: room.tci cap.h base.tci
  cap.h:
  base.tci:
  room $(h): room.tci cap.h base.tci
  room\ $$(h): room.tci cap.h base.tci
  room.h: room.tci cap.h base.tci
  no header
  room.h: room.tci cap.h base.tci
  /* Generated by typec --emit-c-header. Do not edit. */
  room.h: room.tci cap.h base.tci
  elsewhere.h: room.tci cap.h base.tci
  cap.h:
  base.tci:
  room $(h): room.tci cap.h base.tci
  room\ $$(h): room.tci cap.h base.tci
  room.h: room.tci cap.h base.tci
  no header
  room.h: room.tci cap.h base.tci

The rule is written as the header is: it replaces a regular file with
the permissions of that file and nothing else. Both are written before
either is renamed into place, so a refusal of one leaves the other as it
was.

  $ typec -Wno-trigraphs --emit-c-header -MD room.tci
  $ chmod 640 room.h.d
  $ typec -Wno-trigraphs --emit-c-header -MD room.tci
  $ ls -l room.h.d | cut -c1-10
  -rw-r-----
  $ typec -Wno-trigraphs --emit-c-header -MD -MF directory.h room.tci
  typec: directory.h is not a regular file, and typec does not write the dependency rule over it: name another path with -MF
  [81]
  $ ln -s room.h.d link.d
  $ touch -t 200001010000 room.h
  $ for rule in /dev/null link.d; do
  >   typec -Wno-trigraphs --emit-c-header -MD -MF $rule room.tci
  > done
  typec: /dev/null is not a regular file, and typec does not write the dependency rule over it: name another path with -MF
  typec: link.d is not a regular file, and typec does not write the dependency rule over it: name another path with -MF
  [81]
  $ find room.h -newer room.tci

The rule is renamed into place before the header, so a rule that cannot
be renamed, here one made immutable where chflags(1) exists, leaves the
old header, which make then builds again.

  $ immutable() {
  >   typec -Wno-trigraphs --emit-c-header -MD room.tci &&
  >   touch -t 200001010000 room.h && chflags uchg room.h.d &&
  >   typec -Wno-trigraphs --emit-c-header -MD room.tci 2>&1 |
  >     sed 's/: rename: .*/: rename/'
  >   chflags nouchg room.h.d && find room.h -newer room.tci
  > }
  $ if command -v chflags > /dev/null; then immutable; else
  >   echo 'typec: cannot write the dependency rule room.h.d: rename'
  > fi
  typec: cannot write the dependency rule room.h.d: rename
  $ typec -Wno-trigraphs --emit-c-header -MD room.tci -o hand.h
  typec: hand.h is not a header that typec wrote, and typec does not write over it: name another path with -o
  [81]
  $ test -e hand.h.d || echo no rule
  no rule
  $ ls -a | grep '\.tmp$'
  [1]

A makefile that includes the rule rebuilds the header when a file that
the interface includes changes, and nothing when none did. GNU make is
gmake where make is another, as on FreeBSD.

  $ make=$(command -v gmake || command -v make)
  $ printf '%s\n' 'room.h: room.tci' \
  >   '	typec -Wno-trigraphs --emit-c-header -MMD -MP room.tci' \
  >   '-include room.h.d' > Makefile
  $ touch stamp
  $ age() { touch -r stamp cap.h base.tci room.tci room.h room.h.d; }
  $ for cc in "$clang" "$gcc"; do
  >   export TYPEC_CC="$cc" && mv room.h.d old.d && touch room.tci &&
  >   "$make" && age && "$make" -q && echo up to date &&
  >   touch base.tci && "$make" && age &&
  >   touch cap.h && "$make" && age && "$make" -q && echo up to date
  > done
  typec -Wno-trigraphs --emit-c-header -MMD -MP room.tci
  up to date
  typec -Wno-trigraphs --emit-c-header -MMD -MP room.tci
  typec -Wno-trigraphs --emit-c-header -MMD -MP room.tci
  up to date
  typec -Wno-trigraphs --emit-c-header -MMD -MP room.tci
  up to date
  typec -Wno-trigraphs --emit-c-header -MMD -MP room.tci
  typec -Wno-trigraphs --emit-c-header -MMD -MP room.tci
  up to date
  $ export TYPEC_CC="$clang"

